# @SANDBOX@

The only way out of the forge VM. **`dr-forge gateway` GENERATES this** from the
`share/gateway.example/` of Draugr. Generate it again, and do not maintain it. Git does not track
these files, so there is nothing to commit, review, or push.

The VM has **no network adapter**. Its proxy configuration points at its own `127.0.0.1:3128`. An
ssh reverse tunnel carries that to the published port of this mound, `127.0.0.1:18888`. Here,
tinyproxy forwards to the internet through the `sbx` policy of the mound. Thus **the `sbx` policy
is the allowlist**. You manage it with the same tools as each mound: `dr-policy`, and kits.

```text
VM (no adapter) -> guest 127.0.0.1:3128 -> ssh -R over AppSandbox's relay -> Windows 127.0.0.1:18888
  -> this mound: tinyproxy, allow-all -> sbx policy (defaults - .draugr/kit denies + the mixins' allows) -> internet
```

The design, the boundary that it keeps, and the acceptance criteria are in `docs/FORGE.md` of
Draugr.

## What is where

| | |
|---|---|
| `.draugr.conf` | a `shell` mound, 2 GiB, 2 CPUs, ports `18888` → 8888 and `18445` → 445, three kits, the two mounted directories |
| `.draugr/kit/` | installs tinyproxy and samba. `deny:` narrows the `sbx` defaults. Allows **nothing**: each allow is in a mixin |
| `kits/unreal/` | each host that the Epic Launcher, Fab, the Unreal Editor, and Visual Studio needed in tests |
| `kits/base/` | hosts that each forge VM needs, for all purposes: the pinned MSYS2 repository, PyPI and `uv`, AutoHotkey, VS Code, Microsoft-account sign-in |
| `.draugr/hooks/post-up` | the keeper session, tinyproxy, the SMB server of the vault, and a probe |

## Start it

Usually, you do not. `dr-forge up` in the game project starts this mound first through
`DRAUGR_REQUIRES`, then the VM, then the tunnel. From here, `dr-up` starts only this mound. That is
useful when you change only the allowlist.

**Only one gateway can run.** The ports above are fixed, `18022` is pinned, and the tunnel
supervisor holds one mutex. Thus do not start a second gateway beside the first, generated or made
by hand. On Windows, that fails *silently*. A second process can bind a port that is already bound,
and then the two answer in turn.

## Why tinyproxy filters nothing

It has no `Allow` lines and no destination rules, for two reasons. The `sbx` policy is the
allowlist, so a second list here is a second place to keep the same. Also, each client arrives
from the `sbx` forwarder at `172.17.0.10`, so `Allow` cannot tell one from another.

## Add a host

Put it in **its mixin**, not in `.draugr/kit`. Use `kits/unreal/` for an Unreal or Visual Studio
host, and `kits/base/` for something that each Windows VM needs. Then run `dr-up --recreate`,
because a kit change rebuilds the mound.

`dr-policy --allow <host>` opens it on the *running* mound. That is machine state, not repo state.
It is the right tool to find out what a tool wants, and the wrong one to keep it.
If something in the guest fails for no visible reason, read `dr-policy --denied`.

**`deny` wins over `allow` in all places.** No mixin and no `--allow` can open a host in the deny
list of `.draugr/kit`. First delete its line there. Never deny `**`.

## The vault, and the scratch directory

`DRAUGR_MOUNTS` brings two directories into this mound. The guest gets them over SMB on
`127.0.0.1:18445`:

- the **vault holder**, mounted `:ro`, so the kernel refuses a write, whatever the configuration of
  Samba. The VM must not write here. It holds the read-only files that the VM needs, beside the
  Fab cache.
- one **scratch** directory that the guest can write. It is the only writable path across the
  boundary.

The share needs a password. Draugr does not write it, and does not read it. Put a random string in
`~/.ssh/draugr_vault_smb.pass` on the host. That file is often the only record of a password that
the guest also keeps, so nothing in Draugr touches it. Without it, `post-up` says so, and serves no
share. The proxy works in both cases.

**What generation does not do for you:** read access for the user of the mound to those two host
directories. That is host-side ACL work on paths that only you know. It is still the job of the
gateway made by hand. `docs/FORGE.md` lists *equipped* among the jobs that `dr-forge init` does not
do yet. A generated gateway is a correct proxy without it. Only the share waits.

## Which client uses which proxy configuration

The guest needs **three** proxy configurations: the per-user WinINet one, `netsh winhttp`, and the
environment variables. They are separate stores, and no client reads all of them. Tests showed
this:

- `netsh winhttp` serves the services of Windows, installers, and Windows Update, but *not*
  per-user clients.
- .NET and `Invoke-WebRequest` of PowerShell 5.1 read the per-user WinINet value, and ignore
  `netsh winhttp`.
- `curl.exe` takes `-x` or `HTTPS_PROXY`.
- The Epic Launcher and the Unreal Editor use the Windows proxy. Both log
  `HttpProxyAddress = '127.0.0.1:3128'`. The tests did not find which of the three they read,
  because all three were set.
- `winget` stayed broken, for its own reason: `0x8a15000f`, with nothing else denied.

## Trust

Draugr sources `.draugr.conf`, and `post-up` runs on your machine as you. Thus Draugr records a
hash of each, and refuses a version that it did not see. Generation granted that for the two files
that it wrote. If you edit one, run `dr-trust` on it. Until you do, `dr-up` refuses the
configuration and skips the hook.
